ur research\nUC\u2019s signature capabilities\nGroups and centres\nBecome a research student\nWork with us\nResearch Connect\nPartner with UC\nFind an expert\nResources\nFacilities and field stations\nResearch publications\nContact Research & Innovation\nkeyboard_arrow_right\nRongo o te W\u0101\nNews and Events\nkeyboard_arrow_left\nBack\nclose\nClose\nNews and Events\nRongo o te W\u0101\nkeyboard_arrow_right\nNews and Events overview\nLatest News\nNews\nStudent notices\nEvents\nEvents\nRegular events and celebrations\nFuture students\nCurrent students\nInternational\nPostgraduates\nAlumni\nUC Online\nStaff\nHome\nkeyboard_arrow_right\nStudy\nkeyboard_arrow_right\nAcademic study options\nkeyboard_arrow_right\nSubjects\nkeyboard_arrow_right\nmore_horiz\nPostgraduate subject\nMathematical Physics\n22 September 2026\n\u200cCombine advanced mathematics with investigations of the physical world.\nHOW TO APPLY\nApply for a qualification\nCheck your eligibility\nOverview\nMathematical Physics\nThe theories of physics are written in the language of mathematics. Ever since the days of Galileo and Newton, the concepts of physics have also inspired the development of new fields of mathematics. This is still the case today. As we push forward the frontiers of understanding fundamental ideas about the innermost workings of particles, the extremes of black holes, or the origin of the Universe, we are continually confronted by having to invent new mathematics.\nAn education in theoretical physics will give you a deep conceptual understanding of how things work, and how the universe fits together. It will also give you the skills to think from first principles, and to express that thinking analytically in mathematical models. Those skills are highly sought after not only in fundamental research but also in the worlds of industry, finance, education, and the public service.\nWhat will my study involve?\nkeyboard_arrow_down\nMathematical Physics\nTe Kura Mat\u016b | School of Physical and Chemical Sciences has many collaborations both in New Zealand and internationally that give access to some of the best facilities around the world. For example, UC is a member of CERN, the enormous particle accelerator centre in Geneva and also collaborates in the IceCube neutrino experiment at the South Pole.\nUC physicists are currently involved in obtaining fundamental theoretical understandings of cosmology and astroparticle physics.\nCourses\nkeyboard_arrow_down\nMathematical Physics\nSee all postgraduate courses in Mathematical Physics\nUC offers the following postgraduate programmes in Mathematical Physics:\nBachelor of Science with Honours\nPostgraduate Certificate in Science\nDoctor of Philosophy\nSee the individual qualification pages for more information on degree requirements.\nBachelor of Science with Honours majoring in Mathematical Physics\nThe Mathematical Physics major in the BSc(Hons) includes MAPH480 Mathematical Physics Research Project (or SCIE481 and SCIE482) and another 90 points of electives, of which 30-45 points are to be chosen from MATH 401-443, and the remainder from PHYS 411-430 and ASTR 421-425.\nPostgraduate Certificate in Science majoring in Mathematical Physics\nYou will need to complete at least 60 points in courses chosen from 400-level MATH courses, 400-level PHYS courses, and 400-level ASTR courses for the major. A minimum of 30 points must be chosen from MATH 401-443.\nPostgraduate Diploma in Science majoring in Mathematical Physics\nYou will need to complete at least 120 points, including MAPH480 (or SCIE481 and SCIE482) and a further 90 points, 35-40 of which are chosen from MATH401-443, and the remainder from PHYS411-430, and ASTR421-425.\nMaster of Science in Mathematical Physics\nYou will need to complete:\nAt least 120 points in courses including MAPH480 (or SCIE481 and SCIE482), and a further 90 points, 35-40 of which are chosen from MATH401-443 and the remainder from PHYS411-430, and ASTR421-425 for the major\nThe MAPH690 thesis.\nDoctor of Philosophy (PhD) in Mathematical Physics\nIn the PhD, you will complete a thesis of original research in the Mathematical Physics field (MAPH790 Mathematical Physics PhD).\nCareer opportunities\nkeyboard_arrow_down\nMathematical Physics\nMathematical Physics graduates are highly sought after in fundamental research, and also in the worlds of industry, finance, education, and the public service.\nBrochures\nPostgraduate Prospectus 2027\nWhakatairanga T\u0101ura\nDownload\n(application/pdf,\n7 MB)\ndownload\nDownload\nInternational Prospectus 2027\nDownload\n(application/pdf,\n8 MB)\ndownload\nDownload\nQualifications\nBachelor of Science with Honours\nPostgraduate Certificate in Science\nDoctor of Philosophy\nContact us\nSchool of Physical and Chemical Sciences\nScholarships\nScholarships\nRelated subjects\nChoose an area that you are interested in and learn how UC\u2019s range of study options can let you study what you want to.\nMathematics\n29 April 2026\nStudy postgraduate Mathematics; a living subject that is constantly evolving.\nMathematics\nStudy postgraduate Mathematics; a living subject that is constantly evolving.\nMathematics\nPressing this button will open your email client.\nPressing this button will start a call.\nMathematics\nStudy postgraduate Mathematics; a living subject that is constantly evolving.\nPhysics\n29 April 2026\nModern Physics contributes to major advances in technology.\nPhysics\nModern Physics contributes to major advances in technology.\nPhysics\nPressing this button will open your email client.\nPressing this button will start a call.\nPhysics\nModern Physics contributes to major advances in technology.\nclose\nClose\nPrivacy Preferences\nBy clicking \"Accept All Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to enhance site navigation, analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts.\nAccept all\nDecline all\nCookies policy\nBack to top\nexpand_less\nFind it\nContact us\nAcademic s